```config
author: Me
description: A few examples of ways to use NR Markdown notebooks
var: main_app = "TaT (production)"
```

```cell_config
title: Busiest app, last week
var: top_app
chart: billboard
attributes: gauge, style(circular)
```
```nrql
FROM Transaction
SELECT count(*)
FACET appName
SINCE 1 week ago
LIMIT 4
```

### Or, you can display results as a table

```cell_config
title: Busiest app, last week (table)
var: top_app_copy
chart: table
height: 7
query: hide
```
```nrql
FROM Transaction
SELECT count(*)
FACET appName
SINCE 1 week ago
LIMIT 4
```

```cell_config
title: Metrics for the busiest app
var: busiest_metrics
chart: line
attributes: donut, lines, smoothed
```
```nrql
FROM Metric
SELECT count(*)
WHERE appName = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}'
SINCE 1 months ago
TIMESERIES 1 DAY
COMPARE WITH 1 month ago

```

## Overlaying a regression line

This Lua cell reads `busiest_metrics` and adds a least-squares linear regression of `count` against time for the current period only. Previous-period rows are excluded from both the calculation and this chart. The source result is left unchanged.

The current period needs at least two distinct time buckets. The chart uses unsmoothed lines so the fitted trend remains straight.

```cell_config
title: Metrics for the busiest app with regression
query: hide
chart: line
attributes: lines, smoothed
```
```lua
local source = assert(results.busiest_metrics, "Run the metrics query first")
assert(#source.rows > 0, "The metrics query returned no data")

local samples = {}
for _, row in ipairs(source.rows) do
  if row.comparison ~= "previous" then
    assert(type(row.beginTimeSeconds) == "number", "Regression needs time buckets")
    assert(type(row.count) == "number", "Regression needs numeric counts")
    samples[#samples + 1] = row
  end
end
assert(#samples >= 2, "Regression needs at least two current time buckets")

-- Use relative days and centered values to avoid squaring epoch timestamps.
local origin = samples[1].beginTimeSeconds
local meanTime, meanCount = 0, 0
for _, row in ipairs(samples) do
  meanTime = meanTime + (row.beginTimeSeconds - origin) / 86400
  meanCount = meanCount + row.count
end
meanTime, meanCount = meanTime / #samples, meanCount / #samples

local covariance, variance = 0, 0
for _, row in ipairs(samples) do
  local x = (row.beginTimeSeconds - origin) / 86400 - meanTime
  covariance = covariance + x * (row.count - meanCount)
  variance = variance + x * x
end
assert(variance > 0, "Regression needs distinct current time buckets")
local slope = covariance / variance

local rows = {}
for _, row in ipairs(samples) do
  local copy = {}
  for key, value in pairs(row) do copy[key] = value end
  copy.comparison = nil
  local x = (row.beginTimeSeconds - origin) / 86400
  copy.Regression = meanCount + slope * (x - meanTime)
  rows[#rows + 1] = copy
end

emit({ nrql = source.nrql, rows = rows, facets = source.facets })
```

## Hidden results

Since we can bind resulsts for later use, it's possible that you may want to run a query to use later but not care about seeing that intermediate result.

In those caess, you can use `chart: none`. If you also collapse the query section, only the cell header will be shown in the document.

```cell_config
title: Transactions for the busiest app
var: transactions
chart: none
```
```nrql

FROM Transaction SELECT count(*) AS count WHERE appName = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}' SINCE 1 week ago
```

## Seeing "raw" NRQL results

Alternatively, you may not need to see a chart visualized, but rather would like to see the data yourself in-line. For that you can use the `json` chart type.

```cell_config
title: Errors for the busiest app
var: errors
chart: json
```
```nrql

FROM TransactionError SELECT count(*) WHERE appName = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}' SINCE 1 week ago
```

## Running lua scripts

In this exmaple, two previous results (which have hidden graphs, but still run and save results) are referenced to build a result from the independent data sets.

The `emit` function is used to push data into the chartting system.

```cell_config
title: Transactions vs. errors
chart: bar
```
```lua

emit({
  rows = {
    { facet = "Transaction", count = results.transactions.rows[1].count },
    { facet = "TransactionError", count = results.errors.rows[1].count },
  },
})
```

## Running Nerdgraph queries

We can run arbitrary Nerdgraph queries with a light GraphQL editor. Binding of results can be done generically with a `var: var_name` keyword or can be done with a short lua `key = value` accessor. 

```cell_config
title: Entity Search
var: entity_search
var: entities = data.actor.entitySearch.results.entities
chart: none
```
```graphql
query FindEntity($account_id: Int!, $query: String) {
  actor {
    account(id: $account_id) { name }
    entitySearch(query: $query) {
      results { entities {
        guid 
        account { name id }
        name
        reporting
        tags { key values }
      }}
    }
  }
}
```
```variables
{
  "account_id": {{ config.accounts[1] }},
  "query": "name = '{{ results.top_app.rows[1].facet }}'"
}
```

```cell_config
title: Entity tag count
chart: billboard
```
```lua

local entity = assert(results.entities[1], "No matching entity found")

emit({
  rows = {
    { ["Tag Count"] = #entity.tags }
  }
})
```

## Using config variables

This query example shows how to use config block and global variables. In the config block, variables can be added which can be referred to as `config.var_name`

There are two intrinsic config variables as well:
* `config.accounts` (array, remember lua in 1-indexed!)
* `config.api_key` (only the name, not the key itself)

```graphql
query FindEntity($account_id: Int!, $query: String) {
  actor {
    account(id: $account_id) {
      name
    }    
    entitySearch(query: $query) {
      results { entities {
        guid
        name
      }}
    }
  }
}
```
```variables
{
  "account_id": {{config.accounts[1]}},
  "query": "name = '{{ config.main_app }}'"
}
```
